Q: Checkbox Tree for React using Material-UI and Formik. REACTJS A: <p>The bug you're hitting usually comes from the state shape rather than the cascade logic. The fix is a single rule:</p>
<p><strong>Store only checked <em>leaf</em> IDs. Never store a folder's checked state.</strong></p>
<p>A folder's state is derived from its descendants, so if you store it, you now have two sources of truth that can disagree — and they will, the first time a child changes without the parent being updated. Derive it instead and it can't drift.</p>
<pre><code>import { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;

const tree = {
  id: &quot;root&quot;,
  label: &quot;All&quot;,
  children: [
    { id: &quot;a&quot;, label: &quot;Alpha&quot;, children: [{ id: &quot;a1&quot;, label: &quot;A1&quot; }, { id: &quot;a2&quot;, label: &quot;A2&quot; }] },
    { id: &quot;b&quot;, label: &quot;Beta&quot; },
  ],
};

const leavesOf = (node) =&gt;
  node.children?.length ? node.children.flatMap(leavesOf) : [node.id];

// &quot;checked&quot; | &quot;unchecked&quot; | &quot;indeterminate&quot;, computed, never stored
function stateOf(node, checked) {
  const leaves = leavesOf(node);
  const on = leaves.filter((id) =&gt; checked.has(id)).length;
  if (on === 0) return &quot;unchecked&quot;;
  if (on === leaves.length) return &quot;checked&quot;;
  return &quot;indeterminate&quot;;
}

function toggle(node, checked) {
  const next = new Set(checked);
  const leaves = leavesOf(node);
  const turningOn = stateOf(node, checked) !== &quot;checked&quot;;
  leaves.forEach((id) =&gt; (turningOn ? next.add(id) : next.delete(id)));
  return next;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Rendering it — note <code>stateOf</code> is called fresh on every render, so a parent can never be stale:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function Node({ node, checked, onToggle }) {
  const state = stateOf(node, checked);
  return (
    &lt;div style={{ marginLeft: 16 }}&gt;
      &lt;label&gt;
        &lt;input
          type=&quot;checkbox&quot;
          checked={state === &quot;checked&quot;}
          ref={(el) =&gt; el &amp;&amp; (el.indeterminate = state === &quot;indeterminate&quot;)}
          onChange={() =&gt; onToggle(node)}
        /&gt;
        {node.label}
      &lt;/label&gt;
      {node.children?.map((child) =&gt; (
        &lt;Node key={child.id} node={child} checked={checked} onToggle={onToggle} /&gt;
      ))}
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}

export default function CheckboxTree() {
  const [checked, setChecked] = useState(() =&gt; new Set());
  return (
    &lt;Node node={tree} checked={checked} onToggle={(n) =&gt; setChecked((c) =&gt; toggle(n, c))} /&gt;
  );
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>The gotcha that catches everyone:</strong> <code>indeterminate</code> is a DOM <em>property</em>, not an HTML attribute. You cannot set it from JSX — <code>&lt;input indeterminate={true} /&gt;</code> does nothing. You need the <code>ref</code> callback above (MUI's <code>&lt;Checkbox indeterminate /&gt;</code> handles this internally, which is why it works there and not with a native input).</p>
<p><strong>For Formik</strong>, keep the whole selection as one field rather than one field per checkbox:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;Formik initialValues={{ permissions: [] }} onSubmit={console.log}&gt;
  {({ values, setFieldValue }) =&gt; {
    const checked = new Set(values.permissions);
    return (
      &lt;Node
        node={tree}
        checked={checked}
        onToggle={(n) =&gt; setFieldValue(&quot;permissions&quot;, [...toggle(n, checked)])}
      /&gt;
    );
  }}
&lt;/Formik&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Two notes on scale, since this is O(subtree) per render: it's fine into the low thousands of nodes. Past that, memoize <code>leavesOf</code> per node and virtualize the rows, or the recursive render becomes the bottleneck.</p>
<p>Also worth checking before you build any of this: MUI X's Rich Tree View has since added <code>checkboxSelection</code> with <code>selectionPropagation</code> for parents and descendants natively. If you're on a recent version, that may be all you need.</p>

Q: Alternative to $scope in Angular 2.0 A: <p>This code reflects practices from the latest version of Angular,<br />
it uses patterns introduced by signals since v21.</p>
<p>A <code>signal</code> watches a specific value and notifies all subscribers of the latest value when it is updated.</p>
<p>There is no need for <code>takeUntilDestroyed</code> or <code>ngOnDestroy</code> because Angular automatically cleans up signal dependencies.</p>
<p>I used computed here, <code>computed()</code> allows us to return a modified and read-only version of the original signal.</p>
<ul>
<li>We have a SharedDataService
<ul>
<li>It has a signal <code>_sharedDataService</code> this contains an object with various properties that we might want to use &amp; update.</li>
<li>In this example it has a computed <code>message</code> that returns the <code>message</code> property of the <code>_sharedDataService</code> signal</li>
<li>It also has a method <code>updateMessage</code> that updates the <code>message</code> property of the <code>_sharedDataService</code> signal</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>We inject SharedDataService in <code>MessageUpdater</code> component and can call <code>updateMessage</code> to update the message</li>
<li>We also inject <code>SharedDataService</code> in <code>MessageDisplay</code> component and can read the <code>message</code> from the <code>message</code> computed property.</li>
</ul>
<p>We can see with signals how simple it is now to share data between components.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { Component, signal, Service, computed, inject } from '@angular/core';

export type SharedData = {
  message: string;
}

// shared-data-service.ts
@Service()
export class SharedDataService {
  private readonly _sharedDataService = signal&lt;SharedData&gt;({
    message: 'Hello from SharedDataService!',
  });

  message = computed(() =&gt; this._sharedDataService().message);

  updateMessage(newMessage: string) {
    this._sharedDataService.set({ message: newMessage });
  }
}

// message-updater.ts
@Component({
  selector: 'app-message-updater',
  template: ` &lt;button (click)=&quot;newMessage()&quot;&gt;Send New Data&lt;/button&gt; `,
})
export class MessageUpdater {
  private _sharedDataService = inject(SharedDataService);

  newMessage(): void {
    this._sharedDataService.updateMessage('Updated Data from Sender!');
  }
}

//message-display.ts
@Component({
  selector: 'app-message-display',
  template: ` &lt;p&gt;Current Data: {{ message() }}&lt;/p&gt; `,
})
export class MessageDisplay {
  private _sharedDataService = inject(SharedDataService);

  message = this._sharedDataService.message;
}

// app.ts
@Component({
  imports: [MessageDisplay, MessageUpdater],
  selector: 'app-root',
  styles: [],
  template: `
    &lt;app-message-display/&gt;
    &lt;app-message-updater/&gt;
  `,
})
export class App {}
</code></pre>
<p>Summary of Modern Angular Changes (v2–19 vs. v22+)</p>
<div class="s-table-container"><table class="s-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Category</th>
<th>Pre-Angular 20 (v2–19)</th>
<th>Modern Angular (v22+)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>State Management</strong></td>
<td><code>BehaviourSubject</code>, <code>@Injectable</code>, <code>rxjs</code></td>
<td><code>Signals</code>, <code>signal</code>, <code>computed</code>, <code>@Service</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Standalone Components</strong></td>
<td><code>standalone:true</code></td>
<td><code>standalone by default, does not need to be specified</code></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
